# Response headers for the static export (Cloudflare Pages / Netlify syntax).
#
# The JSON-LD API routes and the OG-image routes export as extensionless files
# (inherent to statically exporting route handlers + metadata images), so the
# host can't infer their content type from a file extension — set it here.
# This also (re)applies CORS: the baked static files don't carry the
# Access-Control-Allow-Origin header the original route handlers set, and the
# JSON-LD graph is meant to be fetched cross-origin by agents.
#
# The homepage Link header (RFC 8288) advertises the API catalog to an agent
# that fetches `/` without reading HTML — the catalog is the extensionless
# `/.well-known/api-catalog` route, so its content type is set here too.
#
# `Vary: Accept` marks the pages the Worker content-negotiates: a request for
# `text/markdown` gets that page's Markdown twin from the same URL (see
# src/lib/markdown-negotiation.ts). It is declared on the HTML side for
# correctness in any cache that honours it; the Worker additionally sends the
# Markdown branch `Cache-Control: no-store`, because Cloudflare's own cache
# keys on Accept-Encoding rather than Accept.

# Baseline security headers, site-wide (S6 in plans/audit-2026-08.md). The
# OAuth consent screen sets its own stricter set in worker/oauth.ts and is
# unaffected by this file; everything the static export serves had none.
#
# `nosniff` is the one that matters here. This site serves several
# extensionless routes whose content type is asserted below rather than
# inferred, and content sniffing is exactly the mechanism that overrides such an
# assertion.
#
# `strict-origin-when-cross-origin` is the browser default already, stated
# explicitly so it survives a default changing under us. Claim and dossier URLs
# name what a reader is looking at, so the full path is worth not leaking
# off-origin.
#
# No CSP. Next's static export inlines its bootstrap scripts, so a policy strict
# enough to be worth having needs per-build hashes, and getting it wrong breaks
# the page rather than logging a warning. Probed separately; the three headers
# here do not wait on it.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains

/
  Link: </.well-known/api-catalog>; rel="api-catalog", </schema/v0.json>; rel="service-desc", </about>; rel="service-doc"
  Vary: Accept

/about
  Vary: Accept

/claims/*
  Vary: Accept

/dossiers/*
  Vary: Accept

/.well-known/api-catalog
  Content-Type: application/linkset+json; charset=utf-8
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

/api/graph
  Content-Type: application/ld+json; charset=utf-8
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

/api/claims/*
  Content-Type: application/ld+json; charset=utf-8
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *

/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/claims/:id/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png

/dossiers/:id/opengraph-image
  Content-Type: image/png
